X>Šà•¤Jr/ÖoáiRó[Eò‘m½¸˜ôE{"url":"/gcsl/language/attributes/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/attributes/","content":"Attributes. An attribute goes before a function and says how to run it, for example whether a build step may be cached or how long it may take. GCSL checks every attribute. Most are read by the tool that runs the program, such as a build system, and GCSL itself acts on its own two, retry and timeout. Copy#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true} #[hermetic] fn compile(source: String) -> String { \"compiled {source}\" } #[hermetic(false)] #[timeout(10m)] fn deploy(target: String) -> String { \"deployed {target}\" } result = deploy(compile(\"main.c\")) Declaring an attribute # An attribute's arguments form a record, so you declare an attribute as a record type marked #[attribute]. Each field is an argument, and a field with a default is an argument you can leave out: Copy#[attribute] type device = {name: String, count: Int = 1} #[device(name = \"kvm\")] fn boot() -> String { \"booted\" } booted = boot() Arguments must be constants: literals, or lists and records of them. A field may be an Int, Float, Bool, String, Duration or Size, or a list or record of those. An attribute is declared, imported and named in the same way as any other type, so a tool usually provides its attributes in a module that you import. Using an attribute # Write #[NAME], or #[NAME(ARGUMENTS)], on the line before a function: a fn, a def, or a definition made with a definition type. An attribute with one field can take its argument by position, as in #[timeout(10m)]; one with more fields takes its arguments by name. A misspelt attribute is an error, so a mistake isn't silently ignored. GCSL provides two attributes of its own, retry and timeout: AttributeArgumentMeaning #[retry(times)] times: Int. If the call fails, run it again, up to this many more times. #[timeout(limit)] limit: Duration. Fail the call if it runs for longer than this. A retried call runs its whole body again with the same arguments, so any effects it has happen again: use retry only where that's safe. Retrying a pure function can't change its result, so GCSL warns about it. When a call times out, a program it's running is stopped. A function's own timeout counts as a failure that its retry can retry, and each attempt gets the full time. A timeout of an enclosing call is never retried by a call inside it. Copy#[retry(2)] #[timeout(30s)] fn fetch(url: String) -> String { exec([\"echo\", \"fetched {url}\"]).stdout } page = fetch(\"https://example.com\") Attributes on a definition type # Attributes on a definition type apply to every definition made with it. A definition's own attribute of the same name replaces the definition type's: Copy#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true} #[def_type] #[hermetic] def check(name: String, f: fn(): Bool): fn(): Bool { f } def check fast() { true } #[hermetic(false)] def check online() { true } results = [(check fast)()(), (check online)()()] Here fast is hermetic and online is not.","word_count":487,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Attributes"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"declaring-an-attribute","text":"Declaring an attribute #","location":90},{"element":"h2","id":"using-an-attribute","text":"Using an attribute #","location":210},{"element":"h2","id":"attributes-on-a-definition-type","text":"Attributes on a definition type #","location":409}]}},"word_data":{"more":[{"position":252,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":296,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"out":[{"position":128,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":353,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"constant":[{"position":155,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"as":[{"position":43,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":105,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":193,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":247,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":365,"weight":24,"orig